As we look back upon the recent riots in the UK where the dust has begun to settle on its consequences, we can find within the origin of the violence and even within the response from the government some concerning trends. Trends which have been present for a long time and which rear their ugly head with alarming regularity. The first one is the more obvious one, a trend of misinformation misleading the people of the UK to believe that all of their problems are caused by immigrants or the EU, creating a problem which cannot be solved. A fear mongering, strengthened by foreign influences, and paired with solutions that do more damage than good, leading to a vicious circle of distrust and mismanagement.The second however is more hidden, a natural reaction to the spread of hate and racism; It is a tendency to stifle discussion, to push the rioters away as mere thugs and their concerns as a vile ideology not worth listening to. It is a tendency to close our ears and pretend that these are not our people, paired with the necessary oppression that lets us ignore the harsh reality; These are our people, as misguided as they might be, as vile as their morality might have become, these people are as much our responsibility as their victims. We cannot simply ignore them, nor can we force them to change their views.

To begin, I feel that I must discuss the problems which caused the conflict to erupt. The disinformation being spread online and the years of political campaigning which have planted the seeds of dissent. The falsehood which sparked the riots is but the tip of the iceberg. There was a general sense of unrest caused by years of misdirection, false promises and scapegoating which have made the British public so susceptible to the rallies of hate. British politicians have continuously sought easy scapegoats to explain away their troubles at home and they found them within the EU and within immigrants. But their proposed solutions such as Brexit have done nothing to assuage fears or ameliorate the situation, they have only made things worse. The economy immediately started to struggle with companies pulling out of London as well as massive labour shortages leading to logistical failures. All while desperate immigrants kept using flimsy boats to try and cross the channel in hope of reaching the UK. In the end, even the freedom from EU rules was given up because separating Britain from its most important economic partners would have been disastrous for the already struggling economy. The UK was forced to keep complying with EU laws and trading standards, but had now lost the ability to influence them. Developments that seemed to strategically play into the favour of the Euro-sceptics, as they continued to gain power through a politics of fear. Great Britain has been taken hold by a political philosophy, which Timothy Snyder describes in “The Road to Unfreedom” as the politics of eternity; where one nation is placed at the centre of a cyclical story of victimhood. Time is no longer a line into the future, but a circle that endlessly returns the same threats from the past.

The problems of immigration and of national sovereignty are of course not fully fictional. There have been difficulties with integrating new immigrants into our social fabric and with teaching them about some of the fundamental values such as equality of men and women. And there have been elements of overreach within the EU, as well as guidelines which sought to micromanage and a general lack of effective democratic representation within EU structures. However these problems were overblown to the point where, even if improvements were made, people did not register those improvements. The perception of the issues became detached from the issues themselves as the media kept highlighting only negative stories in order to sell through controversy and politicians such as Nigel Farage kept hammering on vaguely threatening narratives with only some anecdotal evidence to back it up. Immigrants themselves became the problem, rather than their lack of integration and the EU itself became the enemy, rather than the undemocratic structure of the EU. As such, they became problems which could not be fixed; Immigration was always going to exist, even irregular immigration, and the EU was not going to just fall apart out of nowhere. No matter how much border control there was or how hard Brexit was going to be, these issues would continue to be used as scapegoats for all the other domestic problems, such as crime or a stagnating economy. 

All of this was exacerbated by foreign influences from Russia, who sought to disrupt the trust of the people in our institutions by spreading misinformation and especially by weaponizing doubt. In the end, it was never the goal of these bad actors to make people believe their lies. The only thing they needed was to sow doubt and imply that they weren’t the only ones lying, everyone was. It is a technique that we have seen used by multiple far-right movements such as Donald Trump, the Brexit Leave campaign and by Putin himself. These actors try to portray themselves as simply asking legitimate questions, while implying foul play and undermining trust in neutral institutions such as research facilities, expert advisory groups, police and our judiciary. By making everyone suspect, no one became capable of knowing the truth and we became paralyzed by doubt, unable to fix our own problems or even formulate solutions. It brought us towards a state of victimhood, where we were unable to build a better future and we became apathetic towards our own politics. And as the people gave up on their own future and their own responsibilities, they became susceptible to the idea of a politics of eternity. They became susceptible to the idea that we had eternal enemies, which caused all of our suffering. Due to this narrative, thousands of people got swept up in a current of hatred and violence, culminating in the riots over the stabbings of three young children in Southport.

The response to the riots was swift and strict. Sir Starmer’s experience as a prosecutor with the 2011 riots helped him find a good balance between the heavy hand of the law and maintaining due processes. Despite the strain put on Britain’s system of justice by needing to quickly process hundreds of cases, they were completed both swiftly and fairly by the judiciary. Those who received harsher punishments of up to three years in prison were clearly guilty of extreme and inexcusable forms of violence, such as attacking police officers and trapping them in their vehicle. The response worked well to dissuade others from joining and should rightfully be praised as an effective upholding of the rule of law against an onslaught of hate and violence. Even though we speak of fast-tracking court cases, in reality every court case was given time and attention and it was only through an extraordinary effort of keeping the courts open and continuing to work on these cases day and night, that the UK government was able to process them at such high speeds. However, there were some cases which concerned me upon reading into them and those were the cases of two men, Jordan Parlour and Tyler Kay, who were jailed for posting comments online.

To be clear, I do not wish to imply that social media messages should not lead to legal punishments or that freedom of speech allows for calls to violence. Regardless of the circumstances, it is clear that those who were convicted were indeed guilty, since their messages called directly for assaulting and burning hotels and residences of refugees. However, the harshness of their punishment does not seem to fit their role within the greater scheme of events. They were not the first to speak about violence against these hotels, they were not the ones to spread disinformation or to cause the outbreak in any way. They were men who jumped on the bandwagon after the riots were in full swing and while their influence seem to be minimal at best with one of them being convicted for a post with only 6 likes, they were seemingly held responsible for the whole event, used to set an example to everyone else, based only on the flimsy argument that the post could have been shared with more people. Their malicious intent and racial prejudice might have been clear, but there was no real evidence of their effect on the actions of other people. They were portrayed as the causes of the riots, when in reality, it is far more likely that they were just people swept up in the eventsAnd despite that, the punishment for these men was heavier than it was for some people who actively took part in the events.

Now perhaps this may seem to most as relatively innocuous and in truth, it is but a minor complaint towards an overall admirable response at a moment of great crisis. However, it is something which worries me more profoundly because of the history within the UK, and also within the wider world, that tends to overreact to social media messaging, seemingly because of a frustration with the difficulty of catching the main culprits. This time as well, those who were truly responsible for spreading misinformation and causing a surge of hatred and violence appear to have been elusive, foreign interference and as such have not been brought before the law. The primary example of such an overreaction that borders on injustice is the communications act of 2003. This law, which set out to hold people responsible for spreading hate online, created a subjective standard of punishment that threatens the fundamentals of free speech; it outlawed messages which were “grossly offensive”. A term vague enough to include anything that prosecutors might dislike and a principle which forces moral interpretations of the state on individuals. It is no surprise that such a law could lead to absurd outcomes such as a woman being arrested for posting the lyrics to a rap song. And in effect, this law has led to thousands of arrests each year (3359 in 2017), while in other European countries such as Belgium or France, such a thing is a very rare occurence. Even though only 1696 of them actually get charged, this is still a massive amount that has only grown in recent years. Even in Russia, known for its strict media control, only 411 people got officially charged with criminal offences due to social media posts in 2017, which has increased to only 794 cases in 2023, despite a renewed crackdown on anti-war sentiment. Of course, Russians are aware of their government's repressive nature, countries such as France have tried to implement stricter laws on online expression and UK citizens likely get in legal trouble exactly because they believe they are free to express those opinions. However, even then, such a disconnect between what citizens believe would be illegal and what they actually get charged for would be a very problematic development, showing that the laws do not correspond with the citizen’s idea of justice.

The primary problem is that what constitutes ‘offensive’ is very dependent on the eye of the beholder and is dependent on a moral compass which differs between people. In addition, the distinction between simply offensive and grossly offensive, muddies the waters even more, leaving a lot of the details on when and how it is enforced up to the inherently biassed interpretation of the judge and prosecutor. These two factors reinforce themselves and lead to a system of not just double standards, but indeed utter chaos. This kind of arbitrariness will further erode people’s trust in our institution and push people more towards extremism. The inherent problem within this law stems from the politician’s failure to recognize law and morality as separate issues. There are forms of perceived immorality which we, both as voters and as politicians, must allow to exist within the confines of the law, because it is not for us to judge them. After all, law exists for all of us and binds us in the same way, but morality should be allowed to remain diverse, lest we devolve into a state of tyranny, where we force the opinion of the majority upon everyone else. Each individual has their own conscience and must be protected in doing what they believe to be their duty, against the influence of authority and majorities, custom and opinion.

It is through the conflict of different moralities within a strict rule of law, which protects them, that we can improve upon our views of morality. Through discussion and the cultural exchange of various customs, we can find new, better ways to live together as a society, without one man imposing their ideas upon others through violence. To seek any more than the bare minimum of law needed for an orderly, safe and just society, to seek to impose certain moral qualities on all men and women, can lead to no other result than a tyranny of the majority. Throughout history, we have seen all kinds of different sets of moral values co-exist and interact as well as the dangerous and bloody consequences of trying to enforce one’s moral views upon others. In the 17th and 18th century, we learned from the bloody path of religious wars and embraced the freedom of conscience. In the 20th Century,we have seen the horrors of sweeping ideologies, such as fascism and communism, and afterwards have sought to protect our democracies from such extremism. But within the long years of peace, we seem to have forgotten that our co-existence with people from various cultures and ideologies is based on the simple fact that we are allowed to disagree and that liberal democracy is meant to be the means through which we resolve our conflicts peacefully. If, in response to the dangers of far right extremism, disinformation and politics of eternity, we move towards a system of arbitrarily oppressing those dissenting voices, then we risk destabilising the very foundations of our western civilizations.

“Men cannot be made good by the state, but they can easily be made bad. Morality depends on liberty”, Lord Acton

I hope that politicians within the UK will be aware of these principles and that they will seek out better, more intelligible laws which aren’t as open to interpretation for the threat that comes out of current social media. I hope that they will ensure that men who they disagree with are still judged fairly for their actions and not condemned for having the wrong opinions. I hope that they work towards a framework of law which establishes guilt on the basis of general principles, rather than relying on an interpretation of morality. Because, regardless of whether the intent is racist, calling on others for violence has always been wrong and there is no particular need to treat racism as fundamentally different from any other acts of illegitimate discrimination and violence. Simply because we consider such acts against minorities to be even more immoral does not mean we should punish the same acts more harshly. For the sake of the impartiality of our justice systems, it is imperative that we keep our political fight against extremism separate from court proceedings.

Both the far-right campaigning through disinformation and desensitising our voters and our own natural tendency to punish those we see as immoral as harshly as possible constitute dangerous threats to a liberal democracy. Both of them undercut the rule of law and the authority of our justice systems by either denying freedom exists or by seeking to deny freedom itself to our political enemies. As liberals, we must be vigilant against both these harmful tendencies and protect the core principles of our society. We must not allow the long period of peace we have known during our lifetime to lull us into a sense of security. Liberal democracy as we know it must be protected, both from our enemies and from ourselves. This is why we must be aware of our own history and of the reasoning behind our institutions, our cultural values and our political processes.